{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"math","title":"Mathematics","group":"","order":2,"html":"<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> 5<br><strong>Output:</strong> 15<br><strong>Explanation:</strong> 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = 15</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># simple solution\n\ndef findSum(n) :\n\tsum = 0\n\tx = 1\n\twhile x &lt;=n :\n\t\tsum = sum + x\n\t\tx = x + 1\n\treturn sum\n\n# Driver code\nn = 5\nprint(findSum(n))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Solution\ndef findSum(n) :\n\treturn n * (n + 1) / 2\n\n# Driver code\nn = 50\nprint(findSum(n))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># To find the sum of first n natural numbers that avoid overflow if the result is going to be within limits.\n\n# Returns sum of first n natural numbers\ndef findSum(n) :\n\tif (n % 2 == 0) :\n\t\treturn (n / 2) * (n + 1)\n\n# If n is odd, (n+1) must be even\n\telse :\n\t  return ((n + 1) / 2) * n\n\n# Driver code\nn = 51\nprint(findSum(n))</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3>Count Digits</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p>Input: 256<br>Output: 3<br>Input: 75<br>Output: 2<br>Input: 5<br>Output: 1</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>re = 0; x = 9253\nwhile x &gt; 0:\n  re = re + 1\n  x = x // 10\nprint(re)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Palindrome Number</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p>I/P = 78987<br>O/P = 78987</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def ispal(n):\n  rev = 0\n  x = n\n  while x != 0:\n    rev = rev * 10 + x % 10\n    x = x // 10\n  return rev == n, x\n\nprint(ispal(12321))\nprint(ispal(42322))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Factorial Number</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p>I/P = 3<br>O/P = 6</p>\n</div>\n<p>3! = 3 * 2 * 1</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Recursive Approach\n\ndef func(n):\n  if n == 0:\n    return 1\n  return (n*func(n-1))\n\nprint(func(3))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Iterative Approach\n\ndef fact(n):\n  if n &lt; 0:\n    return 0\n\n  elif n == 0 or n == 1:\n    return 1\n\n  else:\n    fact = 1\n    while n &gt; 1:\n      fact *= n\n      n -= 1\n    return fact\n\nprint(fact(3))\n\n# Aux Space : O(n)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Trailing Zeros in Factorial</h3>\n<p>we given a number. The task is to find the Number of Trailing Zeros in the factorial of the Number</p>\n<p>Trailing Zeros are the Zeros, which appear at the end of a number</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> 50<br><strong>Output:</strong> 1 // 5! = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def find(n):\n  if n &lt; 0:\n    return -1\n\n  count = 0\n  i = 5\n  while n // i &gt;= 1:\n    count += n // i\n    i *= 5\n\n  return count\n\nprint(find(100))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>GCD or HCF of two Numbers</h3>\n<p>GCD : Greatest Common Divisor</p>\n<p>HCF : Highest Common Factor</p>\n<p>It is largest number that divides both of them</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> a = 20; b = 28<br><strong>Output:</strong> 4</p>\n</div>\n<p>Factors</p>\n<p>20 : 1,2,4,5,10,20</p>\n<p>28 : 1,2,3,4,7,14,28</p>\n<p>Among all greatest factors we have 4 is common</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Approach\n\ndef  find(a,b):\n  result = min(a,b)\n\n  while result &gt; 0:\n    if a % result == 0 and b % result == 0:\n      break\n    result -= 1\n  return result\n\nprint(find(20,28))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Euclidean Approach : Recursive Approach\n\ndef find(a,b):\n  if b == 0:\n    return a\n  return find(b,a % b)\n\nprint(find(20,28))\n\n# Aux Space : O(log(min(a,b)))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Optimised Euclidean Approach : Iterative Approach\n\ndef find(a,b):\n  while a != b:\n    if a &gt; b:\n      a = a - b\n    else:\n      b = b - a\n  return a\n\nprint(find(20,28))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>LCM of two number</h3>\n<p>LCM : Least Common Multiple</p>\n<p> It is the smallest numner that can be divided by both Numbers.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> a = 15; b = 20<br><strong>Output:</strong> 60</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Approach\n\ndef LCM(a,b):\n  res = max(a,b)\n\n  while True:\n    if res % a == 0 and res % b == 0:\n      return res\n    res += 1\n\nprint(LCM(15,20))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Solution\n\ndef GCD(a,b):\n  if b == 0:\n    return a\n  return GCD(b,a % b)\n\ndef LCM(a,b):\n  return abs(a * b) / GCD(a,b)\n\nprint(LCM(15,20))\n\n# Aux Space : O(log(min(a,b))</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Check for Prime</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> 11<br><strong>Output:</strong> True</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Approach\n\ndef prime(a):\n  if a == 1:\n    return False\n  for i in range(2,a):\n    if a % i == 0:\n      return False\n  return True\n\nprint(prime(11))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Approach\n\ndef prime(a):\n  if a &lt;= 1:\n    return False\n  if a &lt;= 3:\n    return True\n  if a % 2 == 0 or a % 3 == 0:\n    return False\n  i = 5\n  while i * i &lt;= a:\n    if a % i == 0 or a % (i + 2) == 0:\n      return False\n    i = i + 6\n  return True\n\nprint(prime(10))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Super Efficient\n\ndef prime(n):\n  if n == 1:\n    return False\n\n  if n == 2 or n == 3:\n    return True\n\n  if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n    return False\n\n  i = 5\n  while i * i &lt;= n:\n    if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n      return False\n    i = i + 6\n  return True\n\nprint(prime(11))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Prime Factorization</h3>\n<p>Prime factorization is finding which prime numbers multiply together to create the original number. It involves breaking down a number into its prime number components.</p>\n<p>100 : 2^2 * 5^2</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> 100<br><strong>Output:</strong> 2 2 5 5</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def is_prime(num):\n    if num &lt;= 1:\n        return False\n    for i in range(2, int(num**0.5) + 1):\n        if num % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef prime_factors(n):\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        if is_prime(i):\n            while n % i == 0:\n                print(i, end=' ')\n                n //= i\n\nnumber = int(input(\"Enter a number: \"))\nprint(\"Prime factors of\", number, \"are:\", end=' ')\nprime_factors(number)\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>All Divisors of a Number</h3>\n<p>Given a natural number n, print all distinct divisors of it.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> n = 10<br><strong>Output:</strong> 1 2 5 10</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef Div(n):\n  for i in range(1,n+1):\n    if n % i == 0:\n      print(i, end = ' ')\n\nDiv(10)\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Better than Naive Solution : Unsorted Way\n\nimport math\n\ndef Div(n):\n  i = 1\n  while i &lt;= math.sqrt(n):\n    if n % i == 0:\n      if n // i == i:\n        print(i,end=' ')\n      else:\n        print(i,n//i,end=' ')\n    i += 1\n\nDiv(100)\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Better than Naive Solution : Sorted Way\n\ndef print1(n):\n  i = 1\n  while i*i &lt; n:\n    if n % i == 0:\n      print(i, end=' ')\n    i += 1\n  while i &gt;= 1:\n    if n % i == 0:\n      print(n//i, end=' ')\n    i -= 1\n\nprint1(100)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Sieve of Eratosthenes</h3>\n<p>It is about to find all the prime numbers less than or equal to a given number.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> n = 10<br><strong>Output:</strong> 2 3 5 7</p>\n</div>\n<p>Naive Solution: Try dividing with all the number s</p>\n<p>Efficient Solution:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Divisors always appear in pairs.</li>\n<li>One of the divisors in every pair is smaller than or equal to sqrt(n).</li>\n<li>Print all divisors but not necessarily in order.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Solution\n\ndef pr(n):\n  for i in range(2, n+1):\n    if prime(i):\n      print(i, end = ' ')\n\nprint(pr(7))\n\n# Aux Space : Theta(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Approach\n\ndef seive(n):\n  if n &lt;= 1:\n    return\n  isprime = [True] * (n+1)\n  i = 2\n  while i * i &lt;= n:\n    if isprime[i] == True:\n      for j in range(i * i, n+1, i):\n        isprime[j] = False\n\n    i += 1\n  for i in range(2,n+1):\n    if isprime[i]:\n      print(i, end = ' ')\n\nseive(50)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Computing Power</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> x = 2, n = 3<br><strong>Output:</strong> 8</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Naive Approach\n\ndef power(x,n):\n  pow = 1\n\n  for _ in range(n):\n    pow *= x\n  return pow\n\nprint(power(2,3))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Efficient Approach\n\ndef power(x,y):\n  temp = 0\n  if y == 0:\n    return 1\n  temp = power(x,int(y/2))\n  if y % 2 == 0:\n    return temp * temp\n  else:\n    if y &gt; 0:\n      return x * temp * temp\n    else:\n      return (temp * temp) / x\n\nprint(power(2,3))\n\n# Aux Space : O(log(n))</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3>Iterative Power (Binary Exponentiation)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Every number can be written as sum of powers of 2.</li>\n<li>We can travelers through all bits of a number in O(log n)</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\">\n<div class=\"callout-label\"><i class=\"ti ti-terminal-2\"></i> Case Example</div>\n<p><strong>Input:</strong> x = 3, y = 5<br><strong>Output:</strong> 243</p>\n</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code># Iterative Power\n\ndef power(x,n):\n  res = 1\n  while n &gt; 0:\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n      x = x * x\n      n = n // 2\n    else:\n      res = res * x\n      n = n - 1\n  return res\n\nprint(power(3,5))\n\n# Aux Space : O(1)</code></pre></figure></section>"}